--TEST--
`Core\Config::get` with a secret directive's name the program computed throws a `LogicError`, because only a literal name gives the `secret string` type — `rule:config/a-secret-is-a-file-whose-content-is-the-value`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The name reaches `get` through a parameter, so it is not a string literal.
// A secret value read this way would be a plain `?string`, so the call throws.
class Settings {
    public static function read(string $name): ?string {
        return Core\Config::get($name);
    }
}

try {
    echo Settings::read("db.main.password") ?? "unset", "\n";
    echo "the secret was read as a plain string\n";
} catch (LogicError $why) {
    echo $why->message, "\n";
}

// A name that is not secret reads the same way it does as a literal.
echo "memory=", Settings::read("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
--EXPECT--
Core\Config::get(): `db.main.password` is a secret setting, so the call must write its name as a string literal and the result is a `secret string`
memory=128M
